{
  // Fallow configuration for Nextly
  //
  // Fallow reads the whole module graph at once and reports what nothing
  // reaches: unused files, exports and dependencies, unresolved imports,
  // import cycles, duplication and complexity. It answers questions ESLint
  // cannot, because ESLint sees one file at a time.
  //
  // Commands:
  //   - pnpm fallow       = full-repo report, apps/playground included.
  //   - pnpm fallow:audit = the diff, against origin/main. Same check the
  //                         agent gate runs before a commit.
  //
  // CI runs neither: .github/workflows/code-hygiene.yml scans the whole
  // repository against a baseline built from the pull request's base.
  //
  // The lists below exist because a static reader cannot see every way this
  // repo reaches code: a path spelled inside a string, a package pulled in by
  // a bundler rather than an import, a subpath resolved through an exports
  // map. Each entry names one of those, and says which mechanism it is —
  // an entry without a reason is indistinguishable from a silenced bug.
  //
  // Documentation: https://docs.fallow.tools

  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fallow-rs/fallow/main/schema.json",

  // Trees that ship out of this repo rather than run inside it.
  //
  //   templates/ = source that create-nextly-app copies into a new user
  //     project. `templates/blog` is an overlay onto `templates/base`, and
  //     base holds the tsconfig that defines `@/*`, so a specifier like
  //     `@/components/PostGrid` resolves only once the CLI has merged the
  //     two. Read in place, every one of them is unresolvable.
  //   docs/ = MDX for nextlyhq.com/docs, rendered by the site, not imported.
  //   worktrees/ = AGENTS.md mandates a worktree per branch. Placed inside the
  //     repository rather than beside it, each is a full second copy that
  //     fallow walks as ordinary source, so every file, export and cycle is
  //     counted twice and the report is dominated by another branch's tree.
  "ignorePatterns": ["templates/**", "docs/**", "**/worktrees/**"],

  // Reachable only through a string, so the graph has no edge leading to
  // them. Left out, each reports as unused, and an unused file takes whatever
  // it imports down with it.
  "entry": [
    // admin-css ships no vitest config, so its suite runs on the default
    // include, which covers .mjs. These execute on every `pnpm test`.
    "**/*.test.mjs",
    // Spawned by the webServer command in e2e/playwright.config.ts.
    "e2e/scripts/*.mjs",
    // Handed to the Tailwind CLI by packages/ui/scripts/build-css.mjs.
    "packages/ui/src/styles/index.css",
    // Passed as an argv path to the admin-css bin by its integration test.
    "packages/admin-css/__fixtures__/**",
  ],

  // Specifiers tsc and Node both resolve, and fallow does not.
  //
  //   nextly/field-catalog = a declared exports subpath of the nextly
  //     package. Its `types` and `import` conditions point at different
  //     paths (dist/field-catalog.d.ts and
  //     dist/collections/fields/catalog.mjs); fallow follows neither. Drop
  //     this once its exports resolver handles that shape.
  //   ../dist/cli.mjs and ../dist/render/index.js = the only two specifiers
  //     in the tree that reach build output directly — create-nextly-app's
  //     bin shim and one page-builder spec. Present after `pnpm build` and
  //     absent on a clean checkout, so gating on them would make the verdict
  //     depend on build order.
  //
  // Every entry is an exact specifier rather than a prefix. These match the
  // raw import string, so `../dist/**` would also swallow a typo like
  // `../dist/render/missing.js`, and `nextly/field-catalog/**` a deep import
  // below a subpath that exports nothing — the class of mistake this check
  // exists to catch.
  "ignoreUnresolvedImports": [
    "nextly/field-catalog",
    "../dist/cli.mjs",
    "../dist/render/index.js",
  ],

  // NO `ignoreDependencies` HERE, deliberately. It matches by exact package
  // NAME across the whole analysis and suppresses the unlisted-dependency
  // finding as well as the unused one, so naming `cmdk` to excuse admin also
  // excuses a future package that imports `cmdk` without declaring it — which
  // is the missing-manifest case this gate exists to catch. Measured on fallow
  // 3.15.0: nothing narrows it. A per-package `.fallowrc.jsonc` is not read
  // during a root run, and an `overrides` entry scoped to
  // `packages/admin/package.json` (or `packages/admin/**`) leaves the finding
  // in place, because overrides re-severity rules over SOURCE files and a
  // manifest finding is not one of those.
  //
  // The attribution does the job instead, and does it better. Both the local
  // gate and the workflow run new-only against a base, so a dependency finding
  // that predates the change is reported as inherited and does not block;
  // a genuinely new one is not excused by a name and does block. Verified: with
  // no suppressions at all the audit reports 40 findings and still exits 0,
  // every one attributed to the base rather than to the change.
  //
  // Why those findings exist, so the next reader does not go looking for a bug:
  //
  //   packages/admin — the Radix, lexical, cmdk, tailwind-variants and
  //     tailwindcss-animate entries are never imported by admin/src. They
  //     arrive through @nextlyhq/ui and are inlined by the NO_EXTERNAL_DEPS
  //     list in packages/admin/tsup.config.ts, so a consumer installs
  //     @nextlyhq/admin rather than two dozen UI libraries. Remove one and the
  //     bundle loses the component that needed it.
  //   packages/ui — @tailwindcss/cli is run as a shell command by
  //     scripts/build-css.mjs, which is how the theme is compiled.
  //   packages/telemetry — its tsup config sets noExternal: [/(.*)/], so every
  //     dependency is inlined. env-paths arrives through `conf`, which uses it
  //     to pick the OS-appropriate config directory.
  //   packages/plugin-page-builder — @tanstack/react-query is a peerDependency
  //     mirrored into devDependencies so the peer resolves for this package's
  //     own build and type-check.
  //   e2e — the adapter and plugin entries are declared so turbo's
  //     `dependsOn: ["^build"]` builds them before Playwright boots the
  //     playground, which does use them. Without the declaration the suite
  //     starts against a stale or missing dist.
}
